\end_layout

\end_inset

at room temperature
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2007therise"

\end_inset

.
 It was not long before it was realised that the thin silicon dioxide substrate,
 which was relied upon to reveal single layer graphene under the optical
 microscope
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "blake2007makinggraphene"

\end_inset

 and insulate graphene from the silicon back gate
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2005twodimensional,zhang2005experimental"

\end_inset

, was breaking up the 2D electron gas in graphene through surface roughness,
 small areas of inhomogenous charging in the silicon dioxide surface and
 impurities.
 The device performance was significantly improved by inserting a thin layer
 of insulating hexagonal boron nitride (hBN) between the graphene and the
 substrate.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2010boronnitride"

\end_inset

This heralded a step change in graphene device quality and was one of the
 first instances of a van der Waals' heterostructure.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2013vander"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
hBN is an insulating isomorph of graphene, with strong in-plane ionic bonds
 in place of graphene's covalent ones.
 Bulk hBN glues inert sheets of hexagonal boron and nitrogen lattices together
 with van der Waals' bonds, similar to graphite.
 When thin sheets of graphene are placed on top of hBN and annealed, surface
 contaminants pool together in bubbles in what has become known as the self-clea
ning phenomenon.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lui2009ultraflat,haigh2012crosssectional,georgiou2013vertical"

\end_inset

 Self-cleaning arises from the van der Waals' attraction between hBN and
 graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "khestanova2016graphene"

\end_inset

 and results in large areas of atomically clean interfaces, proving vital
 for high quality measurements.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2010boronnitride"

\end_inset

 These simple properties have allowed some of the most exciting physical
 phenomena to be realised, including negative differential conductance
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2013resonant"

\end_inset

, Hofstadter’s butterfly in bilayer graphene 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2013hofstadters"

\end_inset

 and Klein tunneling of the chiral Dirac fermions.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "katsnelson2006chiraltunnelling,young2009quantum,wilmart2014akleintunneling"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These discoveries have largely only been possible due to progress in 2D
 crystal transfer techniques.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kang2012graphene"

\end_inset

 Broadly, there are two methods used to transfer one mechanically exfoliated
 2D crystal on top of another: dry transfer and wet transfer, although other
 techniques are possible for different synthesis routes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2016progress"

\end_inset

 Wet transfer uses a sacraficial water soluble polymer layer to transfer
 2D flakes.
 The sacraficial layer is spin coated onto a silicon substrate and then
 further coated with polymethyl-meth-acrylate (PMMA).
 Graphite is mechanically exfoliated onto this polymer stack and thin flakes
 identified by optical microscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "blake2007makinggraphene"

\end_inset

 and Raman spectroscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferrari2006ramanspectrum"

\end_inset

.
 To isolate a suitable flake, the stack was floated on the surface of a
 water bath until the water soluble polyer dissolved, allowing the silicon
 substrate to sink.
 A glass slide with a cut hole in the middle is then manouvered with a micromani
pulator over the flake on the floating PMMA to remove them from the bath,
 all observed under an optical microscope.
 The PMMA is flipped over so that the flake-side is facing the bench.
 The flake is aligned above a target substrate flake (e.g.
 hBN) and the two brought into contact to make a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stack
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The PMMA is then dissolved with acetone and washed away.
 The stack is annealled to > 110 °C to drive off solvents and contamination.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dry transfer is a variation on the wet transfer technique which attempts
 to further minimise contamination and greatly speed up the transfer process.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "withers2015lightemitting,wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

 The wet transfer procedure is followed to the point where a flake is isolated
 on a PMMA membrane above a smaller target flake.
 Once the two flakes are in contact, instead of dissolving the PMMA, the
 PMMA is lifted from the substrate bringing the two flakes with it.
 This step is then repeated for many flakes to build up a bespoke complex
 stack of different 2D materials.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stacks of 2D crystals are made into devices by adding metallic contacts
 to the surface
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2013resonant"

\end_inset

 or edge of graphene sheets
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

; or, in more complex stacks, to the surface of an encapsulating hBN layer
 so that electrons can be injected by tunnelling into buried conductive
 layers
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2012electron,britnell2012fieldeffect,britnell2013stronglightmatter,georgiou2013vertical,withers2014heterostructures,cao2015quality"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

